## Shinkansen supplement

Сохраняю предложенную карточку и её шесть content-обоснований: неизменённый prompt без ответа, ключевое дополнение о скорости, факты по TGV, ICE и Acela, затем двухфразовое объяснение. Добавляю запрошенные class/meta, canonical recipes, source binding и проверку соседей.

### Class и source metadata

**Почему `easy`:** игрок узнаёт национальную систему скоростных поездов среди четырёх сопоставимых железнодорожных систем. Вопрос не требует знать внутреннее устройство поезда или редкую статистику; страна, маршрут и год дают достаточно прямых подсказок, но prompt не произносит ответ.

**Почему source metadata привязана к JapanGov:** поле `source` относится к основному факту вопроса — японской системе и открытию Токио–Осака в 1964 году. Поэтому в нём остаётся основной первичный источник с его точным title, URL и датой личной проверки `2026-10-13`. TGV, ICE и Acela подтверждаются отдельно как заметки вариантов: SNCF Patrimoine, Deutsche Bahn и Amtrak соответственно; эти ссылки не подменяют основной `source` карточки.

### TGV raw context

Новый полный capture SNCF Patrimoine: HTTP 200, body 46,674 bytes, SHA-256 `6b34d0ee4ed1118818d19cebf0d1422012a2a939c8a892c77bd79045b30957e4`; headers 1,309 bytes, SHA-256 `abfddcc5a5544d5cf9fdc0dee21d9db6de1800c3e7a1c39a87403d8c374c9eba`.

Полный абзац о коммерческом запуске TGV Paris-Sud-Est: raw offset `30301`, length `706`, SHA-256 `3bcbde069bb05d0c9b82562918eebfa7c68fc4083b62f973577dcb793c27f0a8`. Он подтверждает Париж–Лион и 27 сентября 1981 года. Парсер: strict UTF-8, HTML `<p>` extraction, HTML character-reference decoding для чтения; raw offsets и hashes рассчитаны по неизменённым bytes. [Официальная страница SNCF Patrimoine](https://patrimoine.sncf.com/2024/10/31/le-premier-tgv-ne-date-pas-de-1981-mais-de-1971/). :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/eaafonasev/.codex/worktrees/question-quality-wave6-01a12b0f/mindbattle/docs/content-audits/question-quality-wave-7/coordinator-continuation-01a13294/capture-only/japan-tgv-sncf-patrimoine/tgv.body" purpose="source"} :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/eaafonasev/.codex/worktrees/question-quality-wave6-01a12b0f/mindbattle/docs/content-audits/question-quality-wave-7/coordinator-continuation-01a13294/capture-only/japan-tgv-sncf-patrimoine/tgv.headers" purpose="source"}

### Acela PDF extraction recipe

Raw PDF was read with pypdf `6.19.0`: `PdfReader(BytesIO(body_bytes))`; select `pages[1]` (PDF page 2); call `extract_text()`; take the substring starting at `Over 69 million guests have traveled on Acela` through the period after `experience on the nation's busiest rail corridor.`; apply `.strip()`; encode UTF-8 without line-break joining or Unicode normalization. Extracted result is 181 bytes, SHA-256 `24aa9f699963f6ed436df7e0f0766cd0f843a55d4dd84b6a61bccb6ca423975`.

The passage is under the section heading “Acela: A Success Story”; immediately before it the page has “ACELA FIRST CLASS” and “FOOD AND BEVERAGE” headings. Short excerpt: “Acela entered service on December 11, 2000. Acela provides a premium rail travel experience on the nation's busiest rail corridor.” That excerpt is 20 words. The complete passage and its surroundings are in the cited capture. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/eaafonasev/.codex/worktrees/question-quality-wave6-01a12b0f/mindbattle/docs/content-audits/question-quality-wave-7/coordinator-continuation-01a13294/capture-only/japan-rail-primary/acela.body" purpose="source"}

### ICE source locator

ICE’s captured paragraph at byte offset `16374`, length `661`, SHA-256 `089326bdb47863d18f4241c777748910b47113102a33fbdce77283dc549466ab` supports the 2 June 1991 introduction. The comparison-table row at offset `20285`, length `185`, SHA-256 `9c719132bbae5259cd2925a5432fe6dd860349233b7287197eb2767ea9e5c63f` supports 280 km/h. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/eaafonasev/.codex/worktrees/question-quality-wave6-01a12b0f/mindbattle/docs/content-audits/question-quality-wave-7/coordinator-continuation-01a13294/capture-only/japan-rail-primary/ice.body" purpose="source"}

### Composite groups and canonical bindings

The exact composite dictionary keys are:

```json
{
  "group1": {
    "prompt": "string",
    "orderedAnswerTexts": ["string", "string", "string", "string"],
    "correctIndex": 0
  },
  "group2": {
    "explanation": "string",
    "orderedAnswerNotes": ["string", "string", "string", "string"]
  }
}
```

Group 1 is the unchanged prompt, ordered answer texts, and key. Its current and candidate SHA-256 are both `efddd626a931e2f8caa816f53e8cf482cd509d93469c4be238d34ea449fa8b9f`. Group 2 is the explanation and all four ordered notes. Current SHA-256 is `82772d3d9f274210e7ac8ddddd620d5b907b2d90bc2bce19716c0a05d02e5d86`; candidate SHA-256 is `909527c75289bfd5eb68990ed014252247d0ba25a7ff3f202ac32eb806694e1c`.

Canonicalization for these hashes: JSON serialized as UTF-8, sorted keys, compact separators, no trailing newline. The current whole-card hash is `02df18114754a2781f9e3c4347f28b00015b51272b06f87826b8a33f2dc6dc41`; candidate whole-card hash is `4c04fc15908358caacb5f5acfaed8c06b3bd34527faaa5f0b24043cef057b569`. Canonical topic binding read-only hash: `src/content/topics/history-japan.json`, 195,739 bytes, SHA-256 `9dc1e6c6b812995be2ec03d537553f551e353686f4f91dd3e4781d01576b4ef6`.

### Semantic neighbor search

I scanned 110 topic files / 11,000 prompts. Queries for the Tokyo–Osaka route, the year 1964 with that route, Japanese high-speed rail terms, and exact system names returned only the target prompt. Broader matches for “high-speed” outside the topic referred to unrelated animal acoustics and aviation. I also checked the neighboring Japan-history prompts for Tokyo rail history and the 1964 Tokyo Olympics.

Neither close neighbor is a direct or inverse duplicate:

- `history-japan-easy-03a-01` asks what transport first linked Tokyo and Yokohama in 1872; this card asks which high-speed system opened Tokyo–Osaka service in 1964.
- `history-japan-easy-02a-04` asks which city hosted the 1964 Olympics; this card asks for a train system.

